    overview:
      "Hermit is aimed at a different problem from most entries here: not isolation from an attacker, but reproducibility. It runs an unmodified x86-64 Linux program under the Reverie ptrace backend and controls the inputs that make tests flaky — thread scheduling, time, random data, CPUID results and selected file metadata — so a failing run can be replayed exactly and a concurrency bug becomes deterministic. That makes it useful for record/replay debugging, reproducible artifacts, chaos-mode concurrency testing and schedule-sensitive bug analysis, and the project ships guides for running it inside Podman or Docker. Two limits matter. It is Linux x86-64 only, and it requires namespaces, parent-child ptrace, seccomp and, for precise scheduler preemption, user-space performance counters; without the counters, CPU-bound workloads get fewer preemption points. It also is not a security sandbox: the determinism boundary and the security boundary are different things. The README for this repository tells you to install from the maintained fork (rrnewton/hermit) rather than from the Meta-labelled copy, which is worth knowing before you pin a build.",
