    overview:
      "Signadot solves a problem that microVM sandboxes do not: how to test one microservice change against real dependencies without copying a twenty-service stack for every pull request. A sandbox is a Kubernetes namespace containing only the services a change touches, and a routing layer, through a service mesh or sidecar, directs requests for those services into it while everything else falls through to the baseline deployment. The result is a real environment in the customer's own cluster, with real databases and queues, created in the time it takes to schedule pods. The 2026 packaging is aimed squarely at agents: coding-agent environments give each task its own ephemeral sandbox so the agent can run the change, and Plans are reusable validation workflows the agent can author and run on every change. Pricing is per sandbox created rather than per minute, $50 free a month and $250 for 100 with 15 concurrent devboxes, and the Business tier caps spend at $2,050 before overages, which makes the worst case legible to a finance team. The trade-off is scope: this is not a place to run untrusted code you did not write, because the isolation is Kubernetes namespaces and RBAC rather than a kernel boundary, and the cluster is yours to secure. For trusted team code and agent-authored changes it is a better fit than a VM fleet, and it is the entry to choose when the dependency graph, not the filesystem, is the thing you need to reproduce.",
