      "Bubblejail is the Linux desktop answer to sandboxing ordinary applications, and it is in this catalog because a desktop sandbox is where plenty of agent work still needs to run. Its design argument is specific: Firejail transparently overlays the real home directory, so users can launch an app unsandboxed and never notice. Bubblejail gives every instance its own separate home, with a services.toml that lists exactly which sockets and resources the sandbox receives, and optional profiles for common applications. Underneath are bubblewrap user namespaces, seccomp rules and an XDG D-Bus filtering proxy, and there is a Qt GUI for people who do not want to edit TOML. It supports sound, D-Bus and device access through services rather than a broad host mount. It is not an agent API and it is not a hypervisor: it is a practical desktop boundary for Linux, best paired with a VM when the code is genuinely untrusted.",
