    overview:
      "Hugging Face ships a Sandbox API inside `huggingface_hub`, and the honest framing is in its own docs: the feature is experimental, and there are two different sandbox kinds with different guarantees. `Sandbox.create()` gives you a dedicated Job, a whole VM, GPU flavors and about six seconds to ready, which is the right choice for untrusted or GPU work. `SandboxPool` packs many sandboxes into one host Job using uid plus Landlock, boots the first host in about six seconds and then hands out further sandboxes in about a network round trip, which is what you want for hundreds of cheap CPU environments such as RL rollouts or evaluation fan-out. Both hand back the same object, so the code does not change: `run` takes a shell string or an argv list, streams stdout and stderr, raises on a non-zero exit unless you pass `check=False`, and supports background processes you can list and kill. Files can be written, read, uploaded, downloaded, listed and stat'ed. A server inside the sandbox is reachable through `proxy_url_for` over HTTP, SSE or WebSocket with per-sandbox capability headers, with no public port to expose. Lifecycle is sensible: a sandbox outlives the process that created it and can be reconnected from any machine with the same token, an `idle_timeout` of ten minutes by default shuts it down when nothing is running, and a fixed 24-hour cap is the backstop. Billing is plain Jobs billing per minute while starting or running, so a dedicated sandbox costs a whole VM and a pooled sandbox shares one. The caveats are the ones the docs state: no pause or snapshot, a shared pool is a same-user trust boundary and not a security boundary between mutually distrusting workloads, and `forward_hf_token=True` hands your HF token to sandbox code, so keep credentials out of these environments.",
