    overview:
      "Wassette comes at agent tooling from a different direction than the rest of this catalog. Instead of isolating a whole machine or container, it makes the tool itself the sandbox: third-party functionality ships as a WebAssembly Component, compiled with the Component Model and WIT interfaces, and Wassette runs it inside Wasmtime with an explicit permission policy for files, network and system resources. That solves a real problem with MCP servers, which normally inherit the host process's privileges and can therefore read anything the agent can read. Wassette is itself a local MCP server: the agent asks it to load a component from an OCI registry, each exported function becomes an MCP tool, and calls route into the Wasm sandbox. Because components are language-agnostic, the same tool can be written in JavaScript, Python, Rust or Go and reused by any MCP client, including GitHub Copilot, Claude Code and Codex CLI. The caveats matter: the README labels the project early development and not production ready, components must use the Component Model rather than plain Wasm modules, and MCP prompts and resources are not supported, only tools. It is also not a place to run arbitrary Linux binaries; the isolation is a language sandbox, and the operating system interface a component sees is whatever the host exposes through WIT.",
