    overview:
      "Agent-Sandbox.nix is the smallest useful thing in this part of the catalog. Instead of a VM or a container, it is a flake that starts any CLI agent under bubblewrap on Linux or sandbox-exec on macOS, with a declared filesystem, package and environment allowlist. The defaults are aimed at the common accident: the agent can read and write the project you launched it from, but not your home directory, not other repositories, and not the network unless you say so; everything it writes elsewhere disappears when it exits. Credentials are meant to be injected through the flake rather than mounted, and templates cover Claude Code and other agent CLIs. The threat model is written more honestly than most: the README says this is not an anonymity boundary, that the agent sees the hostname, hardware and username, that it can read every branch and stash in the git directory, and that with allowNix it can read the whole store. It also warns that the flake lives in the project and the agent can edit it for the next session, and that macOS sandbox-exec is deprecated, though still the only native unprivileged mechanism. Use it when the goal is to stop an agent from destroying your work; use a VM with a throwaway account when the agent is assumed malicious.",
