    overview:
      "Wirken takes the position that a compromised agent is the base case, and builds the gateway around what it may reach. People reach it over the chat platforms they already use; the agent on the other side reads files, calls APIs and runs tools, but every action passes through Wirken first: three-tier permissions, per-channel isolation, a credential vault the model never sees, and a tamper-evident hash-chained audit log written before each tool call. Shell commands default to an ephemeral no-network container and are refused outright if the sandbox is unavailable rather than silently running on the host, and egress is a per-channel grant through a proxy. The model connection is provider-agnostic — frontier APIs, local weights through Ollama, TEE endpoints or an existing ChatGPT plan are interchangeable per agent — and a per-agent budget in USD or model-seconds can alert or refuse past a ceiling. It ships as one static Rust binary with a pinned, hash-checkable installer and signed releases. The caveats: isolation is Docker-level on a shared kernel rather than a microVM, scheduled preset agents run with no human in the loop, Windows feature parity is partial, and five contributors behind a young project carry the security surface. It is the strongest fit for teams that must answer an audit question about what an agent did and be able to prove the answer.",
