    overview:
      "Tracekit Box starts from a blunt assumption: an agent job is untrusted code that will try every way out. Each job therefore gets its own Firecracker microVM with no network card at all. Network, secrets, model calls, commands and code changes are not denied inside the guest; they have to leave through doors on the host, and a Tracekit signer the agent cannot reach decides each one. A request for an unknown host is held until a person approves it. Secrets are injected host-side, so the guest holds a placeholder and prompt injection can at most learn what the proxy already answered. The proxy refuses cloud metadata and private ranges in every address form the project fuzzed, and DNS lookups only happen for allowed names, which closes the tunnelling gap that let an OpenAI agent reach an outside chatbot. When the job ends, `tkbox export` writes one `.tkb` record and its trust file; `tracekit verify` checks the signature and integrity offline, with no access to the host. A GitHub Action runs Claude Code, Codex or a command on a hosted runner, verifies the bundle and uploads it as an artifact. The caveats: v0 with no tagged release, build from source, Linux with KVM only, one signer per host, a Python client rather than a broad SDK, and a verification guarantee that covers what crossed the doors rather than everything the guest did.",
