    overview:
      "Shadow Clone is a small, sharp tool in the same layer as Firecracker and libkrun, with one idea of its own: make forking a running VM cheap enough to treat VMs like shell users. You boot a template VM once with the runtime already warm, snapshot it, then fork copies that map the template's memory copy-on-write. Reads hit the same physical pages; only writes allocate private memory, and KSM plus virtio-balloon keep the idle footprint down. The vendor's numbers are under 20ms per fork, about 160ms when networking and exec are included, and a hundred forks of an 8 GiB template using one to two GiB if the workloads stay quiet. The whole thing is a single Rust binary of roughly 25,000 lines under MIT, run from a CLI against a Linux host with KVM. That narrowness is the point and the limitation. There is no control plane, REST API, SDK, egress policy or per-tenant accounting; it is a VMM and a fork primitive, and you build the tenancy layer around it. The security argument is the familiar one and holds: containers share a kernel, VMs do not, and CoW makes the VM-per-tenant model affordable. It is a young project, so test the fork path under your own workload before trusting the density claims.",
