    overview:
      "Dedalus Machines sells the persistent-VM half of the agent sandbox problem: a machine is a full Linux VM with its own kernel, root access and filesystem, and it stays allocated between calls. The docs are unusually clear about what survives sleep and what does not. The root filesystem persists, including /home, /root, /etc, /usr/local and /var, so packages, source and build caches remain; RAM, running processes, open SSH sessions, forwarded ports and /tmp do not, so an agent must restart daemons and cannot rely on a process continuing. Wake keeps the machine ID and disk but is not a memory snapshot, and reboot takes a filesystem checkpoint while a forced reboot can lose writes that were not yet checkpointed. There is no general snapshot or fork feature. Isolation is the strongest claim here: a VM boundary with a separate kernel, rather than a shared-kernel container. That matters for unreviewed code and adversarial input. Egress is the missing half. The docs describe no allowlist, firewall or credential-swapping proxy, and the site's earlier security page is not mirrored in the current lifecycle docs, so treat network containment as undefined until the vendor documents it. Access is also gated: the API reference requested access when checked. Billing is per second for CPU and memory while awake, sleep is free, and storage continues to bill beyond the allowance. Hobby starts at $0 with a one-time $20 credit and Pro is $20 a month with $20 of credits, but no public rate card gives per-vCPU or per-GB figures, which makes cost forecasting harder than the plan prices suggest. The claimed 50ms boot is a vendor figure for VM boot, not an independently measured cold start.",
