    overview:
      "Sandvault picks the oldest boundary on a Mac and makes it usable: a second, standard user account. The agent runs as that user through sudo -u or SSH, and macOS Seatbelt adds a profile that denies writes everywhere except the sandbox home, the shared workspace and temp directories, denies reads under /Users except the sandbox account and workspace, blocks disk mounting and raw disk and packet-capture devices, and keeps the user and system keychains out of reach. That combination is the point: the account removes your login identity and home folder, while sandbox-exec narrows what the account itself can touch. The workspace at /Users/Shared/sv-$USER is joint access, and sv-clone can materialize a local or remote repository there and wire a git remote back to the original. The agent list is broad, from Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Gemini and pi to herdr for running several agents in one terminal, and optional bridges expose a headless Chrome or Lightpanda browser and an iOS Simulator to the sandbox over localhost. The limits are sharp. It is macOS only, the kernel is shared, network egress is not filtered, GUI applications cannot run inside, Chrome has to give up its own sandbox, nested-seatbelt tools like swift and xcodebuild need their sandbox disabled, and the first build needs administrator rights to create the account and sudoers entry. There are no snapshots, forks, hosted fleet or API. It is the listing for a Mac user who wants a cheap, low-friction boundary around an agent without running a VM, not for a team that needs a hardened multi-tenant runtime.",
